            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Battles in Italy</strong><br>\nFor over 15 years, Hannibal fought the Romans on their home ground, winning battles at Trebia, Lake Trasimene, and his most famous victory at Cannae in 216 BC. At Cannae, Hannibal's strategic genius was clear as he enveloped a much larger Roman force and decimated it. His victories showcased his ability to outthink and outmaneuver the Roman legions, earning him a reputation as one of history's great military commanders.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hannibal's War Tactics</strong><br>\nHannibal was not only known for his daring crossings and surprise attacks but also for his innovative military tactics. He often used the environment to his advantage, understood the importance of morale, and made alliances with local tribes in Italy. His tactics are studied in military academies around the world even today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Turning Point</strong><br>\nDespite his initial success, the tide began to turn for Hannibal after the battle of Cannae. Rome refused to surrender and instead adopted a strategy of attrition, avoiding open battles with Hannibal. They fortified their cities and cut off his supply lines, which slowly weakened Hannibal's army over time and reduced his capacity to wage war.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
