          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Return to Carthage</strong><br>\nIn 203 BC, with Rome threatening Carthage itself, Hannibal was recalled to defend his homeland. He left Italy, thus ending his Italian campaign. Upon his return, he took command of Carthaginian forces and fought the Roman general Scipio Africanus in the Battle of Zama. Despite his efforts, he was defeated, marking the end of the Second Punic War in 202 BC.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Hannibal continued to serve Carthage in various capacities, including as a statesman. He enacted economic and political reforms that helped to rebuild Carthage, but his continued animosity with Rome lingered. Eventually, Hannibal's political opponents in Carthage turned against him, and he fled, spending his last years in the court of a foreign king.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Historical Impact</strong><br>\nHannibal died around 183 BC, under circumstances that remain somewhat of a mystery. His death marked the end of an era for Carthage. Despite his ultimate defeat, Hannibal is remembered as a brilliant military leader whose tactics and battles have been studied by generations. His life and military campaigns have had a lasting impact on the history and military strategy of the Western world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
